		{
			dependencies: ["3A8709849D637D3D"]
			description: [
				"Towers, taverns, mines, temples and buried chambers are not random interruptions. Together they imply generations that mapped routes, guarded resources and failed in different ways."
				"Loot is only the most obvious thing a structure contains. Architecture reveals local materials, traps reveal feared enemies, and abandoned workspaces reveal what earlier people considered valuable."
				"Mark useful sites, restore what deserves a second life, and leave signs at cleared dangers. An explored world should become more legible."
				"Write signs, books, map labels, and albums in plain practical language first. Lore becomes believable when another player can follow the road, reproduce the meal, repair the machine, or recognise the warning it preserves."
			]
			id: "5FCB22913E016C22"
			rewards: [{
				id: "495497C1AE538DD8"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 5
			}]
			tasks: [{
				id: "390527F2DEF7DE05"
				title: "I read and understood these field notes"
				type: "checkmark"
			}]
			title: "The Roads of the Lost"
			x: 2.0d
			y: 4.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["5FCB22913E016C22"]
			description: [
				"The expanded wildlife belongs to ecosystems rather than a universal list of targets. Predators, scavengers, pollinators and strange cave species communicate through movement and sound long before they drop an item."
				"A naturalist asks where a creature lives, what it eats and what threatens it. A careless hunter asks only what it drops and eventually discovers an empty habitat."
				"Sanctuaries, aquariums and careful breeding are another form of progression: proof that the settlement can understand life without immediately consuming it."
				"The Chronicle links mechanics to memory: every portal changes the rules, every structure records a purpose, and every recovered object asks what the settlement will do with its knowledge."
			]
			id: "53986E1D3385AA38"
			rewards: [{
